ircle, footprint read off the ground file (7 m cells) and the rest from its published building history; no photographs were available. The 1916 Beaux-Arts building by Hubbell & Benes in white Georgia marble facing south over the Fine Arts Garden and Wade Lagoon: the hexastyle Ionic portico, the blind wings with pilasters, the gallery skylights, the grand stair down to the garden; Marcel Breuer's 1971 wing to the north in bands of two granites with its cantilevered canopy; Rafael Vi\u00f1oly's 2013 glass-roofed atrium between them and his east and west wings in marble with dark granite bands and glass ends. Guessed: all heights (about 18 m), the skylights, the column count on the portico, the band spacing on the Breuer wing, the garden's layout."}